WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.330 --> 00:00:10.170
When Donald Trump was elected president, British
Euroskeptics rejoiced.

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:14.980
The combination of Brexit and a Republican
administration in Washington were supposed

00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:19.320
to usher a new era for the Anglo-American
relationship, which would be no longer held

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:23.680
back by Brussels or by President Obama’s
reserved attitude towards the UK.

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:26.800
They were in for an unpleasant surprise.

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:30.840
Although Theresa May was the first foreign
leader to visit the White House after the

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:34.550
inauguration, it is fair to say that she did
not succeed in building a good relationship

00:00:34.550 --> 00:00:36.430
with the US president,

00:00:36.430 --> 00:00:41.470
and within days after her visit, a petition against
inviting Donald Trump on a state visit collected

00:00:41.470 --> 00:00:44.220
nearly two million signatures.

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:48.470
As the president finally visits the UK on
a more low-key trip, it is also apparent that

00:00:48.470 --> 00:00:51.430
the two nations have deep substantive differences.

00:00:51.430 --> 00:00:57.190
Whether it is the Iran Deal, trade, or regulation
of tech businesses, the UK finds itself more

00:00:57.190 --> 00:01:00.979
aligned with other EU countries than with
the United States.

00:01:00.980 --> 00:01:05.810
The question then remains: What, if anything,
is still special about the special relationship?

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:10.280
What do you think about US-UK relations?

00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:12.200
Let us know in your comments.

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:15.880
Also, let us know what other topics you’d
like our scholars to cover in 60 seconds,

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:19.780
and be sure to like and subscribe for more
research and videos from AEI.

